-- title:
--   Component ideal sheaves cut out the reduced crossing divisor
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$, an integer $M \neq 0$ with $5 \le M$ and $p \nmid M$, a characteristic-zero field $L$ that is a cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ for $\{p\}$, a primitive $p$-th root of unity $\zeta \in L$, and the intermediate field $K$ of $L \subseteq \mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ equal to [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.x1FunctionField (M * p))`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), i.e. $L$ adjoined to the coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion field of level $\Gamma_1(Mp)$. Let $A$ be a discrete valuation domain with fraction field $L$ such that $p$ lies in its maximal ideal and $\zeta$ is in the image of $A$, with $K$ an $A$-algebra compatibly with $L$, and let $j \in K$ be nonzero with Laurent expansion the image of [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157) under [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb L`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81). Let $k$ be an algebraically closed $A$-algebra field of characteristic $p$, and let $c_1 : C_1 \to \operatorname{Spec} k$, $c_2 : C_2 \to \operatorname{Spec} k$ be proper, smooth of relative dimension $1$ and geometrically integral, with $C_1$, $C_2$ integral. Let $i_1, i_2$ be closed immersions of $C_1$, $C_2$ over $k$ into the base change along $A \to k$ of the two-chart model $\mathrm{TwoChartModel}\,A\,K\,j \to \operatorname{Spec} A$; assume their images cover the geometric special fibre, that the scheme-theoretic intersection $C_1 \times_{X_k} C_2$ is reduced, and that its point set has cardinality $n > 0$. Let $O$ be a discrete valuation domain, $\rho_O : A \to O$ with $\mathfrak{m}_A$ mapping onto $\mathfrak{m}_O$, and $\mathrm{to}\kappa : O \to k$ with $\mathrm{to}\kappa \circ \rho_O = \mathrm{algebraMap}\,A\,k$; let $bc$ be a comparison morphism from the base change of the model along $A \to k$ to its base change along $\rho_O$, compatible with the first projection and, on second projections, with $\operatorname{Spec}(\mathrm{to}\kappa)$. Finally let $z : \mathrm{Fin}\,n \to \operatorname{Hom}(\operatorname{Spec} k, C_1 \times_{X_k} C_2)$ be such that each composite with either projection is a section of $c_1$, respectively $c_2$, and the $n$ induced closed points are pairwise distinct. The conclusion is fourfold, symmetric in the two indices: the ideal sheaf on $\mathrm{pullback}\,c_2\,(\mathbf{1}_{\operatorname{Spec} k})$ obtained by pulling back, along the first projection followed by $i_2$ followed by $bc$, the kernel ideal sheaf of $i_1$ followed by $bc$, is invertible, in the sense that around every point there is an affine open $U$ and $f \in \Gamma(U)$ whose basic open contains the point and on whose affine basic open the ideal is generated by a single non-zerodivisor; and this ideal sheaf equals the product over $i \in \mathrm{Fin}\,n$ of the ideal sheaves $\mathcal{I}$ of the degree-one relative effective Cartier divisors `RelEffCartierDiv.ofPoint` attached to the sections $z\,i$ followed by the second projection, each of which is by definition the kernel ideal sheaf of the graph of that section. The same two assertions hold with the roles of $(1,2)$ exchanged, the product then running over the graphs of the sections $z\,i$ followed by the first projection.
--
--   This is the transversality statement for the two vertical components of the special fibre of the two-chart model of $X_1(Mp)$ over an unramified extension $O$ of $A$: the ideal sheaf of one component restricts, on the other component, to the reduced divisor supported on the $n$ crossing points, each with multiplicity one, and is invertible there. It feeds the subsequent computation identifying the restricted line bundles with a product of point divisors on each component, used in the relative Picard analysis of the model.
